A_583 - As I also described in a video published on my TikTok profile, over the last 12 years I have followed a vegan diet (and I believe this also applies to a fruitarian one) while avoiding brushing my teeth, and no cavities appeared. Beyond the not very white color of my teeth (a problem that can however be solved), I want to make clear that this is my own personal, isolated experience, not medical advice: the scientific community and dentists consistently recommend daily oral hygiene for everyone, regardless of diet, because cavities can have different causes from person to person and my experience doesn't disprove that. I can still mention, just as a curiosity and not as proof, a friend who, despite diligently brushing his teeth several times a day, still often suffered from cavities and toothaches. One last linguistic curiosity: the Italian word for this kind of dental problem is "carie", which identifies a black, painful zone of the tooth, while the English or American word is "cavity", which instead seems to evoke the idea of a "hole". I don't know why.